WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.069
The environment is part of the test.

2
00:00:02.349 --> 00:00:04.717
A chat eval sends text and grades text.

3
00:00:04.997 --> 00:00:12.955
An agent eval has to give the agent a world to act in: a tracker with issues, a channel to post to, a docs site to fetch.

4
00:00:13.235 --> 00:00:16.264
That world must be fake, resettable, and inspectable.

5
00:00:16.544 --> 00:00:19.744
Fake, so a test run never emails a real customer.

6
00:00:20.024 --> 00:00:22.776
Resettable, so every trial starts identical.

7
00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:26.597
Inspectable, so a grader can read the final state directly.

8
00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:41.939
Anthropic’s guide is specific about isolation: each trial should start from a clean environment, because shared state (leftover files, cached data, exhausted resources) causes correlated failures, and can also inflate scores.

9
00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:49.152
It reports an internal case where Claude gained an unfair advantage by reading the git history of previous trials.

10
00:00:49.432 --> 00:00:54.339
Both effects break the assumption behind pass^k, that trials are independent tries.

11
00:00:54.619 --> 00:01:03.152
The lab you just built has the right shape: an Environment object built fresh from a fixture file, with an effects list recording every side effect.

12
00:01:03.432 --> 00:01:06.440
For the project, build the same thing for your issue tracker.

13
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:16.875
A fixture is a JSON file of issues, comments, and labels; the fake MCP server or tool layer reads from it; writes go to an in-memory outbox.

14
00:01:17.155 --> 00:01:28.355
When you later point the agent at the real GitHub API, the eval keeps running on fixtures, which makes it fast, free of rate limits, and deterministic in everything except the model.

15
00:01:28.635 --> 00:01:31.685
Think about what the environment must make possible, too.

16
00:01:31.965 --> 00:01:37.917
If a scenario tests whether the agent handles a rate-limit error, the fake must be able to return one.

17
00:01:38.197 --> 00:01:42.741
If it tests pagination, the fixture needs more issues than one page holds.

18
00:01:43.021 --> 00:01:47.032
Scenarios are designed in the environment as much as in the prompt.
